we'd like to consider velocity-dependent  resistive forces so let's imagine a  situation where we have an object and  it's falling in a gravitational field so  we have a gravitational force and this  object is like a marble falling in a vat  of oil and there is a velocity dependent  resistive force that is acting on this  object and let's again choose a positive  direction down and now for objects that  are moving very slowly in a fluid let's  make a model for our velocity dependent  resistive force will assume that the  resistive force is proportional to the  velocity  it's opposing the direction of motion  and we'll put a coefficient alpha in  front and this is our model for velocity  dependent resistive forces for objects  that are moving very slowly for our case  let's consider the units of the  coefficient alpha so the units of alpha  have the units of force divided by  velocity so the units of force are we  have the units of force divided by the  units of velocity and that gives us  kilogram meter per second squared  divided by the units of velocity which  are velocity per second and so we see  that the coefficient of alpha has the  units of kilogram per inverse second now  now what we'd like to do is apply  Newton's second law so let's analyze the  forces and get the equation of motion  for this object falling in a viscous  medium with a resistive force in the J  hat direction we have the gravitational  force and we have our resistive friction  force which we're writing V Y as the Y  component of the velocity and that's  equal to M times DV Y DT now notice that  the acceleration we're not treating as a  constant it's a the derivative of the  velocity this leads us to our  differential equation which is written  as dy DT I'll divide through by M G  minus alpha over m dy  now this is a linear linear and velocity  first-order differential equation with a  constant term in here which is called in  homogeneous linear first order  differential equation we shall solve  this like we did with our other  equations by separation of variables so  what we do is we bring the velocity  terms to one side and the time terms to  the other side and now we can integrate  both sides directly but before I  integrate this side I'd like to rewrite  it by writing it as DV y and I'd like to  pull out a minus alpha over m and this  term here becomes minus mg over alpha  why did I do this for the integration it  just makes my natural logarithm integral  look a little bit easier and over here I  have DT now what I want to do is  integrate and we're going to integrate  from some initial velocity which we're  going to take to be zero we're gonna  drop this let this object be released  from rest and we're gonna integrate to  some final speed V Y of T over here  let's just call our integration variable  T Prime and we integrate T Prime from  zero to some time T so this is our  variable and the velocity is a function  of time now doing this integral first  off this constant term comes out so we  have 1 over minus alpha over m and the  integral is just a natural log integral  of V Y minus mg over alpha and we have  to divide that in the lower limit V Y is  0 and we have just minus mg over alpha  and this side is very simply T now we  can rewrite this equation by bringing  the M over alpha to the other side and  we have log of V y minus mg alpha over  minus mg alpha equals minus alpha M T  now recall that e to the log of any  quantity X is equal to X so if i  exponentiate both sides  I get B Y minus mg over alpha divided by  minus mg alpha is equal to the  exponential e to the minus alpha M T now  the last step is simply to bring this  over there multiply and do a little bit  of algebra and so I get that the  velocity as a function of y is equal to  mg alpha times 1 minus e to the minus  alpha M T now there's a few interesting  things here that we want to look at  suppose we define this exponential e to  the minus alpha MT as equal to e to the  minus T over tau where tau is M over  alpha the units of tau when we look at  the units it has a units of mass which  is kilograms over the units of alpha  which we worked out before so we have  kilograms over kilogram inverse second  that gives us units of seconds and  that's what we expect because T seconds  over seconds gives us a dimensionless  quantity for the exponential now the  last thing we'd like to look at is let's  just see what happens  when we plot this well if we're plotting  B y as a function of T we start off at  zero and we're going to reach some  terminal quantity V Y terminal which is  when we have a graph like that and we  can see what that terminal speed is when  we set t equal to infinity e to the  minus infinity is zero and so this  terminal speed is equal to just this  coefficient mg over alpha and that's the  terminal speed now notice that as a  check for what we did we can come back  to our differential equation and what  does terminal speed mean notice that  when you get to this limit as T  approaches infinity  the slope of the velocity curve is zero  and that's really the statement over  here that the Y terminal is when the  velocity is no longer changing in time  you can see it graphically and this is  the statement when we go back to  Newton's second law that there's  acceleration is zero so the sum of the  forces at terminal speed are zero the  resistive force is equal to the  gravitational force and so from this  equation here as a check we can see that  V y terminal equals mg over alpha which  checks with our calculation and so we  have confidence that we analyzed this  correctly and that gives us our motion  one last thing that's quite interesting  notice that the terminal speed depends  on mass this coefficient alpha is only a  function of the properties of the object  the size the shape the geometric  properties of the object not how dense  it is so if you have two objects like  two balls same radius but one was denser  than the other then the other one mass  would be heavier and the terminal speed  would be greater so what we see is that  two identical objects one heavier than  the other  the heavier object reaches a faster  terminal speed than the lighter object  which explains a well observed phenomena
 

